# CSS/SCSS Styling Rules

## Absolute Rules

- **Never use `!important`** — fix specificity issues properly
- **Never use inline styles in components** — use CSS Modules or SCSS classes. The one exception is React Flow, which takes edge stroke, background dots and minimap fills as inline styles; those pass a `var(--token)` through (see `docs/theming.md`)
- **TSX structure follows `rules/html-rules.mdc`** — semantic elements, `useTranslations()` for every visible string
- **Never use hardcoded colors** (`#fff`, `#0b57d0`, `rgba(...)`, `hsl(...)`) — always use design token variables. The token layer itself (`tokens/*.json`, `styles/_theme-dark.scss`) is where literal colour is allowed, and nowhere else
- **Prefer semantic tokens over the raw palette** — surfaces, text, borders and accents come from the themed semantic layer (`--surface*`, `--on-surface*`, `--outline*`, `--primary*`, `--on-primary*`, `--error*`, `--inverse-*`) or the domain layer (`--male-*`, `--female-*`, `--root-*`, `--branch-*`, `--canvas-dot`, `--edge-*`, `--union`, `--ribbon`). The raw palette (`--color-white`, `--color-black`, `--color-slate-*`, `--color-rose-*`, …) is **not** overridden in dark mode and will paint light-mode pixels on a dark background. See **Dark Mode**
- CSS variables use `--_` prefix for private/scoped vars (e.g. `--_card-bg`)

## Design Tokens

- All values come from Style Dictionary (`tokens/*.json` → `styles/_css-variables.css` + `styles/_base-variables.scss` + `styles/variables.js`). The generated files carry a "do not edit" header and mean it — change the JSON and run `pnpm build-dictionary` (which `dev` and `build` do for you)
- Use `var(--spacing-*)` for spacing, `var(--border-radius*)` for corners, `var(--box-shadow-base)` for elevation. For typography prefer the typo mixins (see **Typography**); for colour prefer the semantic layer (see **Dark Mode**)
- SCSS breakpoints: `base.$breakpoint-sm` (36rem), `base.$breakpoint-md` (48rem), `base.$breakpoint-lg` (62rem), `base.$breakpoint-xl` (75rem)
- Each breakpoint has a `-max` variant (e.g. `$breakpoint-md-max: 47.9375rem`)

## Dark Mode

- Light values are generated into `styles/_css-variables.css`. Dark is a hand-maintained override in `styles/_theme-dark.scss`, applied by `[data-theme="dark"]` on `<html>` **and** by a `prefers-color-scheme` query for users who chose `system`. Both go through the same `dark-tokens` mixin — add a token to that mixin, never to one branch only
- **Only the semantic and domain layers adapt.** They are the single source of truth for surfaces, text, borders and accents:

  | Use | Token |
  | --- | --- |
  | Page background | `--background`, `--surface` |
  | Card / panel / raised surface | `--surface-container`, `-lowest`, `-low`, `-high`, `-highest`, `--surface-bright`, `--surface-dim` |
  | Translucent panel over scrolling content | `--surface-veil` |
  | Primary text / icon on surface | `--on-surface` |
  | Secondary text / muted icon | `--on-surface-variant` |
  | Further emphasis steps | `--on-surface-soft`, `--on-surface-faint`, `--on-surface-ghost` |
  | Hairline border / divider | `--outline-variant` (weakest: `--outline-subtle`) |
  | Stronger border / outline | `--outline` |
  | Primary fill (button, active toggle) | `--primary`, `--primary-hover`; text on it `--on-primary` |
  | Pale brand-tint background | `--primary-container` (text: `--on-primary-container`) |
  | Error text or icon | `--error`; on a filled error `--on-error`; tinted `--error-container` / `--on-error-container` |
  | Inverted (e.g. tooltip) | `--inverse-surface` / `--inverse-on-surface` |
  | Person cards | `--male-*` / `--female-*` (`-line`, `-soft`, `-ink`, `-solid`) |
  | Chart focus | `--root-*`, `--branch-*` |
  | Canvas | `--canvas-dot`, `--edge-spouse`, `--edge-descent`, `--union`, `--ribbon` |

- **Do not use the raw palette for theme-driven chrome** — `--color-white`, `--color-black`, `--color-slate-*`, `--color-sky-*`, `--color-rose-*`, `--color-emerald-*`, `--color-amber-*` all keep their light-mode value under `[data-theme="dark"]`. A `background: var(--color-white)` reads as a stark white panel on a dark page
- **Narrow exceptions where raw palette tokens are correct** — content that is itself fixed across themes:
  - Image overlays / scrims: `color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-black), transparent X%)`
  - Text or icon sitting on a fixed-colour filled badge
- **`shared.asBox` already paints `var(--surface-container)`** — when you need a different surface level, override the local var: `--_as-box-bgr: var(--surface-container-high);`. Do not hardcode a colour
- **Component-local dark overrides go through `--_` private vars**, not duplicated declarations — the same variable-swap pattern used for `:hover`/`:focus` (see **Responsive**). Reach for this only when a component needs something the token layer can't express; the usual answer is that it should use a different token:

  ```scss
  .spinner {
    [data-theme="dark"] & {
      --_spinner-track: var(--outline);
    }

    border-color: var(--_spinner-track, var(--outline-variant));
    border-top-color: var(--primary);
  }
  ```

- **Native form chrome adapts via `color-scheme: dark`** (already set in `_theme-dark.scss`) — do not restyle scrollbars, autofill, date pickers, or `accent-color` per component
- **Verifying a change is dark-mode-safe**: open the page with `<html data-theme="dark">`. If text/borders/backgrounds disappear or render at wrong contrast, swap the offending token to its semantic equivalent from the table above

## Migrating off Tailwind

The app is moving from Tailwind utilities to CSS Modules, one component at a
time. Both are live, and both read the same custom properties.

- **New and converted components use `.module.scss`.** Do not add Tailwind classes to a component that has a module
- `styles/_compat.scss` aliases the pre-token names (`--panel`, `--ink-muted`, `--line`) onto the semantic layer so the ~250 remaining utility call sites keep working. **Never reference the left-hand column in new code** — use `--surface-container`, `--on-surface-variant`, `--outline-variant`
- When a component's conversion removes the last user of a utility, delete its entry from both `_compat.scss` and the `@theme inline` block in `styles/globals.scss`
- A half-converted component is worse than either — convert all of its markup in one pass

## Cascade Layers

`styles/globals.scss` declares the whole cascade in one line:

```scss
@layer frameworks, reset, base, theme, layouts, components, utilities;
```

Later wins, so precedence is decided there and nowhere else — that is what makes
`!important` unnecessary rather than merely discouraged.

- `frameworks` — third-party CSS (Tailwind's `theme.css` today)
- `reset` — Tailwind preflight
- `base` — element defaults: `html`, `body`, global keyframes
- `theme` / `layouts` — reserved; declared so their position is fixed before anything needs them
- `components` — where component CSS would sit if Next layered it
- `utilities` — Tailwind utilities
- **Custom properties are unlayered on purpose.** Layers order *declarations of the same property*; token definitions never conflict, and keeping them out means `:root` and `[data-theme]` behave predictably

**CSS Modules are emitted unlayered**, and unlayered rules beat every layer. That
is deliberate for the migration — a converted component's styles are not at the
mercy of a leftover utility class — but it means a Tailwind utility cannot
override a module class. Convert a component's markup in one pass and the
question doesn't arise.

## CSS Modules

- **One folder per component**, named for it, holding everything it owns:

  ```
  components/Toolbar/
    Toolbar.tsx
    Toolbar.module.scss
    index.ts          // export { Toolbar } from "./Toolbar"
  ```

  Consumers import the folder (`from "@/components/Toolbar"`), so the
  internal file layout can change without touching call sites. Sibling
  components are imported by alias too: `from "@/components/PersonSearch"`.
- One `.module.scss` per component, colocated with it
- Import as `import styles from "./Component.module.scss"`
- Use `clsx` for conditional classes — never string concatenation for multiple classes
- **Styling another component from outside goes through its `--_` variables**, scoped under an ancestor of yours so the rule wins regardless of which module stylesheet loads first:

  ```scss
  // Toolbar.module.scss — retune Button without touching Button
  &__controls &__control {
    --_btn-padding: var(--spacing-1-5) var(--spacing-3);
  }
  ```

  Never re-declare the other component's properties: it has declared them once
  already, and a second declaration is a specificity race you win by accident.

## BEM Methodology

- Follow BEM (Block Element Modifier) for all class naming and HTML structure
- **Block**: the component root (`.card`, `.panel`, `.toolbar`)
- **Element**: a child part of the block, using `__` (`.card__title`, `.toolbar__group`)
- **Modifier**: a variation or state, using `--` (`.card--deceased`, `.toolbar--compact`)
- In SCSS modules, use nesting with `&`:

```scss
.card {
  &__title { ... }
  &__meta { ... }
  &--deceased { ... }
}
```

- In JSX, reference as `styles.card__title`, `styles['card--deceased']`
- Keep nesting flat — max 2 levels (block > element). Never nest elements inside elements (`&__body__title` is wrong)
- Use data attributes for dynamic states instead of BEM modifiers when toggled by JS: `data-sex="female"`, `data-size="sm"`
- **Use `aria-pressed`, `aria-selected`, or `aria-expanded` for interactive toggle states** — style with `&[aria-pressed="true"]` instead of adding/removing a `.selected` class via JS. This keeps accessibility and styling in sync

## Responsive

- Mobile-first: default styles are mobile, use `@media (min-width: base.$breakpoint-md)` to add desktop styles
- Prefer `@container` queries over `@media` when the component is inside a resizable container
- Container queries require `container-type: inline-size` on a parent
- **Never override property values directly inside state changes or media queries** — instead, declare the property once using a scoped CSS variable (`--_` prefix) with a fallback, then reassign only the variable in the alternate state. This applies to `:hover`, `:focus`, `:active`, `aria-*` selectors, and `@media`/`@container` queries alike:

```scss
// Bad — duplicates property declarations across states
.card {
  background: var(--surface-container);
  box-shadow: var(--box-shadow-base);
  scale: 1;
  &:hover {
    background: var(--surface-container-high);
    box-shadow: 0 16px 24px var(--box-shadow-color);
    scale: 1.02;
  }
}

// Good — single declaration, variable swap
.card {
  --_card-bg: var(--surface-container);
  --_card-shadow: var(--box-shadow-base);
  --_card-scale: 1;

  background: var(--_card-bg);
  box-shadow: var(--_card-shadow);
  scale: var(--_card-scale);

  &:hover {
    --_card-bg: var(--surface-container-high);
    --_card-shadow: 0 16px 24px var(--box-shadow-color);
    --_card-scale: 1.02;
  }
}
```

This also works for child element properties driven by parent state — declare the `--_` variable on the block, consume it on the element, reassign on the block's `:hover`:

```scss
.card {
  --_content-color: inherit;
  &:hover { --_content-color: var(--on-primary); }
  &__content { color: var(--_content-color); }
}
```

## Layout Patterns

- **For single-column stacked layouts, use `display: grid; gap: ...`** — not `display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: ...`. Grid is simpler when you only need vertical stacking with a gap
- Use Flexbox for component internals that need horizontal alignment or wrapping
- When using flex: always set `min-width: 0` on children that need to shrink below content size (inputs, text)
- Use `flex: 0 0 auto` for fixed-size flex children (icons, buttons)

## Units

- Spacing: `var(--spacing-*)` tokens or `rem`. `fn.rem(18px)` converts a one-off value a design hands over in pixels
- **Typography: prefer typo mixins over raw font-size/line-height tokens** — see **Typography**
- Breakpoints: SCSS variables only (`base.$breakpoint-md`), not hardcoded px
- Icon sizes in components: prefer `rem` or numeric px values, avoid `em` (parent-dependent)

## Typography

- **Use `@include typo.*` mixins instead of setting font-size/line-height tokens directly** — mixins set the full type scale (size, line-height, weight, and related properties) and stay aligned with the design system
- Import at the top of the module:

```scss
@use "styles/typo-mixins" as typo;
```

- Available mixins: `typo.ui-body`, `typo.ui-caption`, `typo.ui-small`, `typo.h1`–`typo.h5`, `typo.hero`, `typo.tabular`. Heading mixins accept an optional `$color` arg; UI mixins accept an optional `$bold` arg

```scss
// Bad — only sets font-size; misses line-height, weight, etc.
.label {
  font-size: var(--ui-small-font-size);
}

// Good — full ui-small scale via mixin
.label {
  @include typo.ui-small;
}

// Bold variant
.label {
  @include typo.ui-small($bold: true);
}
```

- Use `typo.tabular` for anything that lines up in a column — dates, ages, generation counts
- Reserve raw `var(--ui-*-font-size)` / `var(--heading-size-*-font-size)` for one-off overrides, not as the default way to style text

## Cross-Browser (Safari)

- **Never use `oklch(from var(...))` or relative color syntax with CSS variables** — Safari doesn't support it reliably; use `color-mix(in srgb, ...)` instead
- **Never rely on `aspect-ratio` alone for square buttons** — set explicit `width` and `height` and zero out padding via custom properties
- **Always add `-webkit-appearance: none` on inputs** — Safari adds default styling that changes dimensions
- **Always add `flex-shrink: 0` on icons and fixed-size elements** — prevents Safari from shrinking them in flex containers

## File Structure

Imports resolve from the project root via `sassOptions.loadPaths` in
`next.config.ts`, so a module anywhere in the tree writes the same paths:

```scss
@use "styles/base-variables" as base;  // $breakpoint-*, generated
@use "styles/typo-mixins" as typo;     // type scale
@use "styles/mixins" as shared;        // asBox, sr-only, ellipsis, truncate, focus-ring
@use "styles/functions" as fn;         // rem()

.component { ... }
```
